From: Alexandr Mironov <7062352+alexandr-mironov@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: sm8150-xiaomi-vayu: enable IPA

The modem itself comes up on vayu: remoteproc loads modem.mbn, rmtfs and
pd-mapper run, and ModemManager reaches the modem over QMI. It then throws
the modem away:

    [qrtr0/probe] probe step: done
    [device qcom-soc] creating modem with plugin 'qcom-soc' and '1' ports
    [base-manager] couldn't create modem for device 'qcom-soc':
                   Failed to find a net port in the QMI modem

There is no net port because IPA, which carries the modem data path, is
disabled: sm8150.dtsi leaves ipa@1e00000 for boards to enable, and the vayu
device tree never does. Everything else is already in place - the board file
relocates ipa_fw_mem and ipa_gsi_mem to match the vayu memory map, so the
reservations the IPA driver needs exist but go unused.

Enable the block the same way sm8150-xiaomi-raphael.dts does. The firmware
image ships in the vayu firmware repository as ipa_fws.mbn.

With this, IPA probes, a rmnet_ipa netdev shows up, and ModemManager keeps
the modem instead of discarding it.

Tested on Xiaomi POCO X3 Pro (vayu_global, M2102J20SG), Huaxing panel,
kernel 7.0.0-sm8150 from sm8150/7.0-wip.

---
 .../boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi     | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8150-xiaomi-vayu-common.dtsi
@@ -696,6 +696,14 @@
 	status = "okay";
 };
 
+&ipa {
+	status = "okay";
+
+	qcom,gsi-loader = "self";
+	memory-region = <&ipa_fw_mem>;
+	firmware-name = "qcom/sm8150/Xiaomi/vayu/ipa_fws.mbn";
+};
+
 &remoteproc_adsp {
 	status = "okay";
 	firmware-name = "qcom/sm8150/Xiaomi/vayu/adsp.mbn";
